--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
The conditional operator's type depends on whether a pointer operand is a null
pointer constant, i.e. an integer constant expression with value 0 (possibly
cast to void *). An expression that merely simplifies to 0 but is not an
integer constant expression (because it reads a runtime variable, uses a comma
operator, ...) is not a null pointer constant; GCC and Clang agree. Conversely
any integer constant expression evaluating to 0 is a null pointer constant,
regardless of the operators used to build it (subtraction, division, shifts,
...). See src/ansi-c/c_typecheck_expr.cpp:typecheck_expr_trinary.
